            j) Idempotency - Duplicate Event Handling - 

               - Worker kisi bhi notification ko aggregator (SendGrid/Twilio/FCM) ko bhejne se pehle check karta hai ki wo notification already bhej to nahi diya gaya. Ye check zaroori hai kyunki duplicate 
                 notification possible hai agar upstream system (Order Service) ne business_events topic me event (evt-123) publish kiya, Kafka broker se ACK bhi mil gaya, but offset commit karne se pehle 
                 wo crash ho gaya. Agar upstream idempotent nahi hai, to restart hone ke baad wo same event_id (evt-123) ko dubara publish kar sakta hai. Ab business_events topic me evt-123 do baar hai  
                 (do alag offsets pe). Notification Instance 1 dono ko process karega, aur dono baar 3 channel topics (email_events, sms_events, push_events) me produce karega → worker ko evt-123:EMAIL, 
                 evt-123:SMS, evt-123:PUSH do baar milenge → bina idempotency ke user ko duplicate email/SMS/push jaayegi. Isliye worker-level idempotency check must hai — ye duplicate scenario
                 rokta hai jo upstream ki non-idempotency ki wajah se create hota hai.

               - Worker topic se message uthata hai (say notification_id = "evt-123:EMAIL"). Aggregator (SendGrid/Twilio/FCM) ko call karne se pehle, Redis me idempotency key check karta hai:

                  Key format: idem:send:{notification_id}
                  Example: idem:send:evt-123:EMAIL

                  Redis GET idem:send:evt-123:EMAIL

                 --------------------------------------------------------------------
                  CASE 1 — Key Does NOT Exist (Fresh Message, Pehli Baar Processing)
                 --------------------------------------------------------------------

                  → Redis GET par key nahi milti (nil), iska matlab notification pehle process nahi hui hai.

                  → Worker Redis mein SET command ke through idempotency key create karta hai aur uska status IN_PROGRESS set karta hai:
                       SET idem:send:evt-123:EMAIL "IN_PROGRESS" EX 3600

                  → Iske baad worker aggregator (SendGrid/Twilio/FCM) ko HTTP REST API call bhejta hai aur configured timeout tak response ka wait karta hai.

                  → Ab aggregator ke response ke basis par 2 sub-cases hote hain:

                     CASE 1 — SUCCESS (MOST CASES MEIN AGGREGATOR 202 ACCEPTED RESPONSE DETA HAI, JABKI 200 OK RESPONSE RELATIVELY RARE HOTA HAI):
                     ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                        STEP 1 : Worker Redis me key update karta hai: SET idem:send:evt-123:EMAIL "SENT" EX 86400 (TTL 24 hours).
                                 SET idem:send:evt-123:EMAIL "SENT" EX 86400

                        STEP 2 : notification_audit table me entry insert karta hai — status = "SENT", sent_at, notification_id, channel, template_id, user_id, language, provider = "SendGrid/Twilio/FCM".

                        STEP 3 :  Iske baad worker corresponding topic mai offset commit karta hai aur next message process karne lagta hai.


                       ## IMPORTANT: 202 Accepted ka matlab hai ki aggregator ne notification request accept karke delivery ke liye queue kar di hai. Iska matlab actual notification abhi user tak deliver nahi hui hai.
                                     Jab aggregator ko actual delivery status milta hai, toh woh webhook ke through Notification Service ko update bhejta hai. Notification Service webhook payload ke basis par 
                                     notification_audit table mein delivery status update karta hai.

                     CASE 2 — FAILURE (5xx / timeout / network error):
                     --------------------------------------------------
                        Agar aggregator 5xx error return karta hai ya connection timeout hota hai, toh retry generally safe hoti hai, kyunki in cases mein aggregator ne request accept nahi ki hoti. 
                        Lekin Read Timeout ke case mein retry risky ho sakti hai, kyunki possible hai ki aggregator ne notification accept kar li ho, lekin slow response ya network issue ki wajah se 
                        acknowledgement worker tak nahi pahucha ho. Aise mein retry karne par duplicate notification send ho sakti hai. Isliye duplicates prevent karne ke liye aggregator side par bhi
                        idempotency mechanism hona chahiye.

                        STEP 1 : Worker Redis se apni idempotency key delete karta hai (DEL idem:send:evt-123:EMAIL) Taaki next retry ke time key IN_PROGRESS state mein na mile. Otherwise, retry worker 
                                 IN_PROGRESS status dekhkar message ko skip kar sakta hai.

                        STEP 2 : Worker configured retry policy ke according Exponential Backoff + Jitter ke saath aggregator API ko dobara call karta hai. Har retry attempt mein Redis mein IN_PROGRESS key 
                                 create hoti hai (SET idem:send:evt-123:EMAIL "IN_PROGRESS" EX 3600). Aggregator se success response milne par Redis mein status ACCEPTED (TTL: 24 hours) set hota hai aur 
                                 notification_audit table mein bhi status ACCEPTED update hota hai. Failure par Redis key delete karke next retry ki jaati hai. Ye process configured maximum retry attempts
                                 tak continue hota hai, jisme har attempt ke beech backoff duration increase hota hai aur jitter add kiya jata hai.

                        STEP 3 : Agar configured saare retry attempts exhaust hone ke baad bhi request fail hoti hai, toh worker Redis mein status FAILED (TTL: 7 days) set karta hai aur notification_audit
                                 table mein FAILED status aur failure_reason update karta hai. Iske baad Kafka offset commit karke worker next message process karta hai. DLT ka flow alag se dekhenge.


                 ---------------------------------------------------------------
                  CASE 2 — Key Exists (SENT / In-Progress / Previously Failed)
                 ---------------------------------------------------------------

                   → Agar Redis GET par key mil jaati hai, toh worker uski value check karta hai. Value ke basis par 3 possible cases handle kiye jaate hain:

                      CASE 1 — VALUE = "SENT" (DUPLICATE — PEHLE HI SUCCESSFULLY PROCESS HO CHUKA HAI)
                      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                       Iska matlab notification pehle hi successfully aggregator ko bheji ja chuki hai. Isliye worker aggregator ko dobara call nahi karta, directly corresponding Kafka topic ka offset commit karta hai
                       aur notification_audit table mein koi change nahi karta, kyunki existing entry already SENT hai. Iske baad worker next message process karna start karta hai. Ye case tab occur hota hai jab 
                       upstream service same business event ko duplicate publish kar deti hai business_events topic mein. Consequently, Notification Service bhi same event ko process karke sabhi relevant channels
                       (SMS, EMAIL, PUSH) ke respective topics par duplicate notifications publish kar deti hai.


                      CASE 2 — VALUE = "FAILED" (RETRIES EXHAUST HO CHUKI HAIN, EVENT DLT MEIN HAI):
                      ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                       Iska matlab hai ki is notification ko bhejne ke saare configured retry attempts exhaust ho chuke hain aur failed message ko notification type ke according respective DLT topic
                       (email_events_dlt / sms_events_dlt / push_events_dlt) mein publish kar diya gaya hai. Ab Ops team decide karegi ki in failed notifications ko kaise reprocess karna hai
                       (DLT flow mein detail mein dekhenge). Is case mein worker aggregator ko dobara call nahi karta, directly corresponding Kafka topic ka offset commit karta hai aur next message
                       process karna start kar deta hai.


                      
                      CASE 3 — VALUE = IN_PROGRESS (WORKER CRASH HO GAYA AUR USKE BAAD KAFKA REBALANCING TRIGGER HO GAYI):
                      -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                      - Iska matlab hai ki pichla worker ne event (EMAIL/SMS/PUSH) uthaya, Redis me IN_PROGRESS status set kiya, aggregator (SendGrid/Twilio/FCM) ko HTTP call bheja — lekin offset commit 
                        karne se pehle crash ho gaya. Ab Kafka ne rebalance kiya, aur naya worker ko wo partition assign ho gaya. Kyunki pichle event ka offset commit nahi hua tha, naya worker ne dubara 
                        same event uthaya.

                      - Naya worker ko pata nahi ki pichla worker ne kya kiya:
                        (a) Aggregator call kiye bina crash kiya
                        (b) Aggregator call kiya but response aane se pehle crash ho gaya
                        (c)  Aggregator call kiya, response aaya:
                             i)  Success aaya → Redis (SENT) aur notification_audit table (SENT) update se pehle crash
                             ii) Failure aaya (configured retries ke baad) → Redis (FAILED) aur notification_audit table (FAILED) update se pehle crash

                       Teeno cases me naye worker ko dikhne wala state identical hai — Redis me IN_PROGRESS, aur notification_audit table me koi row nahi.

                       ## IMPORTANT: Agar worker aggregator ko API call karne ke immediately baad crash ho jaata hai aur aggregator notification successfully deliver kar deta hai, toh webhook ke through status
                                     update aayega. Lekin previous worker ne notification_audit table mein entry insert nahi ki thi, isliye webhook ka UPDATE query zero rows affect karega. Consequently, delivery
                                     status database mein record nahi ho payega. Matlab webhook bhi confirm nahi kar paayega ki notification actually user tak pahunchi thi ya nahi.


                      - Ab worker ke paas sirf ye 2 choice hain:

                        1) Option 1: Skip + Offset Commit

                           → Worker Redis mein IN_PROGRESS state dekhkar notification skip kar deta hai, corresponding Kafka topic ke partition ka offset commit karta hai aur next message process karna start kar deta hai.
                           
                           → Agar previous worker aggregator ko call karne se pehle crash ho gaya tha, toh notification permanently lost ho sakti hai. Lekin agar aggregator request receive karke notification successfully 
                             deliver kar chuka tha, toh koi issue nahi hoga, kyunki notification user ko sirf ek baar hi deliver hui hai.

                        2) Option 2: Skip + Offset Commit

                           → Worker Redis mein IN_PROGRESS state dekhkar key ka TTL expire hone ka wait karta hai. TTL expire hone ke baad Redis key automatically delete ho jaati hai, jiske baad worker aggregator API ko
                             dobara call karta hai. Kyunki worker synchronously wait karta hai, toh TTL expire hone tak uska thread blocked rahega. Isliye IN_PROGRESS key ka TTL aggregator ke expected processing time ko 
                             dhyan mein rakhkar reasonably small configure karna chahiye, taaki unnecessary waiting na ho.

                           → Agar previous worker ki request aggregator ko successfully mil gayi thi aur notification deliver ho chuki thi, toh retry karne par duplicate notification ja sakti hai, provided aggregator 
                             idempotent nahi hai.

                     - Kaunsa option choose karna hai, ye notification_type par depend karta hai —  duplicate notification worse hai ya notification loss? Worker ye decision payload (jo ki Instance 1 ne channel-specific
                       topic — email_events / sms_events /  push_events — me produce kiya tha) me aaye notification_type field ke basis par leta hai.

                         a) OTP: Skip approach prefer kar sakte hain, kyunki user resend kar sakta hai and duplicate OTP confusing ho sakta hai.
                         b) Transaction Alert : Transaction Alert notifications ke liye hum Retry Approach prefer karte hain, kyunki in cases mein notification loss duplicate notification se zyada risky hai.     
                                                Agar transaction alert user tak nahi pahunchta, toh user ko transaction ka pata nahi chalega. Ye particularly important hai jab koi fraudulent transaction hua ho,
                                                kyunki user ko uski timely information nahi mil paayegi.Isliye duplicate notification annoying ho sakti hai, lekin tolerable hai.

                         c) Marketing: Skip approach prefer kar sakte hain, kyunki notification loss acceptable hai, jabki duplicate messages se user irritate hokar unsubscribe kar sakta hai.

                      
